&lt;p>unspecified&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="garden-description">Garden Description&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>We know of a grove of trees in the precinct of the temple of Poseidon, thanks to Pausanias' and Strabo's descriptions of it in the early 1st and the later 2nd century CE (&lt;a href="http://data.perseus.org/citations/urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0099.tlg001.perseus-eng1:8.6.22">Strab. 8.6.22&lt;/a>; &lt;a href="http://data.perseus.org/citations/urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0525.tlg001.perseus-eng1:2.1.7">Paus. 2.1.7&lt;/a>). According to Pausanias, on one side of the precinct stood statues of athletes who had been victorious in the Isthmian games, and opposite them was a row of pine trees. The fact that these trees stood in a row suggests that they were intentionally planted.&lt;/p>
